  "comment": "EIGHTEENTH PACK, AND THE CORPUS'S FIRST SYLLABARY. Ethiopic (Geʽez) writes a CONSONANT AND ITS VOWEL AS ONE SIGN — ሀ ሁ ሂ ሃ ሄ ህ ሆ is one consonant in seven vowels, seven code points and not a single combining mark. So the mark-as-letter law bought for Devanagari is not needed here at all, while `graphemes` meets its limit from the other side: some 270 signs exist and only those alive in the shows are declared. Amharic is SOV and says its copula LAST; the forge's arithmetic template puts the equality word BEFORE the result, and that word order is the forge's, not the language's. Six person cells of eight: the second and third person split by gender (አንተ/አንቺ, እሱ/እሷ) and the forge holds six. TIER 1.",
